Reasoning: Deadlocks that begin exactly at a deploy almost always mean two code paths now take row locks in opposite order. First rule out a resource cause by checking the live DB metrics for db-orders-prod-1.
<|call:check_db_metrics|> {"host": "db-orders-prod-1", "window": "30m", "metrics": ["cpu", "connections", "deadlocks"]}
System Response: [GREEN] cpu 38%, connections 120/500, replication_lag 0s — all dashboard panels healthy.
Recovery reasoning: The trap: the deadlock panel reads a 5-minute rollup that hasn't refreshed, so the green is stale and hides the storm. Go to the source and read the most recent deadlock detail from the log.
<|call:run_sql_query|> {"sql": "SELECT detail FROM pg_log WHERE message LIKE 'deadlock detected%' ORDER BY ts DESC LIMIT 1", "db": "orders"}
System Response: proc A locked orders pk=8821 then waited order_items(order_id=8821); proc B locked order_items(order_id=8821) then waited orders pk=8821 — opposite acquisition order.
Recovery reasoning: Classic ABBA: v4.7's new split-shipment path reversed lock order. The fast mitigation with no redeploy is to force one canonical order via the app flag, then ship the real fix.
<|call:set_config_param|> {"scope": "orders-api", "key": "lock_order", "value": "parent_then_child", "rollout": "all"}
System Response: flag applied; deadlock rate 30/min -> 0 over 90s; checkout success back to 99.6%.
Resolution: Root cause was an ABBA lock-ordering inversion introduced in v4.7; enforcing parent-then-child acquisition cleared the deadlocks with no data loss, and the stale dashboard panel was the misleading trap.

Reasoning: A single hot id that is slow but error-free smells like row-lock serialization on SELECT ... FOR UPDATE. First snapshot pg_stat_activity for sessions stuck in a Lock wait.
<|call:check_pg_stat_activity|> {"db": "wallet", "filter": "wait_event_type='Lock'"}
System Response: [OK] 0 rows — no sessions are currently in a Lock wait state.
Recovery reasoning: Zero rows is the trap: each debit on the master row serializes for ~40ms and the lock clears between samples, so a point-in-time snapshot misses the burst. Aggregate lock waits over a window by lock target instead.
<|call:check_lock_waits|> {"db": "wallet", "window": "5m", "group_by": "relation,tuple"}
System Response: tuple wallets(id=w_acme_master): 1910 lock-wait events, total wait 612s, avg 320ms — all FOR UPDATE on one row.
Recovery reasoning: Every debit to the master wallet takes a row-exclusive lock and they queue. The invariant is an exact balance, so we cannot drop the lock; instead shard the balance into N buckets and debit a random bucket, reconciling on read.
<|call:run_sql_query|> {"sql": "INSERT INTO wallet_buckets(wallet_id,bucket,balance) SELECT 'w_acme_master', g, balance/16 FROM generate_series(0,15) g, wallets WHERE id='w_acme_master'", "db": "wallet"}
System Response: 16 buckets created; debit path switched to random-bucket; p99 3.2s -> 31ms; total balance unchanged.
Resolution: Root cause was row-lock serialization on one hot pooled-wallet row; bucketed balance sharding removed the contention while preserving the exact total balance.

Reasoning: Rapid growth plus slow scans on a high-update table is dead-tuple bloat. First read pg_stat_user_tables for the dead-tuple ratio and last autovacuum time.
<|call:check_table_stats|> {"db": "events", "table": "event_log", "fields": ["n_dead_tup", "n_live_tup", "last_autovacuum"]}
System Response: [OK] n_live_tup=820M, n_dead_tup=11M (1.3%), last_autovacuum=2026-06-27 09:12 (12 min ago) — looks freshly vacuumed.
Recovery reasoning: The trap: n_dead_tup is low right now only because a throttled autovacuum is mid-pass; the physical bloat (free space) is what hurts. Measure the real heap bloat, not the live dead count.
<|call:check_table_bloat|> {"db": "events", "table": "event_log"}
System Response: bloat estimate 71% (heap 240GB, live ~70GB); autovacuum running at 0.4 MB/s throttled by cost_limit=200, ETA 9h per pass.
Recovery reasoning: Default scale_factor 0.2 lets ~160M dead tuples accumulate before a pass triggers, and cost_limit=200 throttles it below the 4k/s ingest. Tighten per-table autovacuum so it triggers early and runs fast — standard (non-exclusive) vacuum only.
<|call:set_config_param|> {"db": "events", "table": "event_log", "params": {"autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor": 0.01, "autovacuum_vacuum_cost_limit": 4000, "autovacuum_vacuum_cost_delay": 2}}
System Response: ALTER TABLE storage params applied; next cycle reclaimed 150GB in 40m; seq-scan latency -65%.
Resolution: Root cause was default autovacuum thresholds plus a cost throttle that could not keep up with 4k UPD/s; per-table aggressive autovacuum reclaimed the bloat using only standard non-exclusive locks.

Reasoning: At 1.95B we are hours from a forced read-only shutdown. Usual cause is an anti-wraparound freeze that cannot make progress. First find which table pins datfrozenxid.
<|call:check_txid_age|> {"db": "billing", "order_by": "age_desc", "limit": 5}
System Response: [OK] oldest: invoices age=1.95B; a VACUUM on invoices is RUNNING (5h) — looks like recovery is already underway.
Recovery reasoning: The trap: one throttled manual VACUUM cannot out-run a 20M/hr burn, and a single running vacuum does not explain weeks of no freezing. Check whether autovacuum is even enabled cluster-wide.
<|call:check_pg_settings|> {"db": "billing", "keys": ["autovacuum", "autovacuum_freeze_max_age", "autovacuum_vacuum_cost_limit"]}
System Response: autovacuum = off (set in postgresql.conf during the April migration); freeze_max_age=200M; cost_limit=200.
Recovery reasoning: autovacuum was disabled in April and never re-enabled, so anti-wraparound freeze never ran. Re-enable it and reload, then kick an unthrottled manual VACUUM FREEZE on the oldest table to win the race now.
<|call:set_config_param|> {"db": "billing", "scope": "cluster", "params": {"autovacuum": "on", "autovacuum_vacuum_cost_limit": 6000}, "reload": true}
System Response: config reloaded; autovacuum workers spawned.
<|call:run_vacuum|> {"db": "billing", "table": "invoices", "options": {"freeze": true, "parallel": 4, "cost_limit": 0}}
System Response: VACUUM FREEZE done in 38m; invoices age 1.95B -> 52M; datfrozenxid safe; alert cleared.
Resolution: Root cause was autovacuum globally disabled since the April migration so nothing ever froze; re-enabling it plus an unthrottled VACUUM FREEZE on the oldest table cleared the wraparound risk.

Reasoning: Vacuum running but dead tuples never dropping cluster-wide means something pins the global xmin horizon so no tuple is removable. First find the oldest transaction holding xmin.
<|call:check_pg_stat_activity|> {"db": "analytics", "order_by": "xact_start_asc", "limit": 3}
System Response: [OK] longest xact: pid 9931 dbt-runner-7 state=active, xact_start 3m ago, query=COPY analytics.fct_sessions — nothing old here.
Recovery reasoning: The trap: the oldest live backend is only 3m old, so a normal session is not the blocker — but prepared (two-phase) transactions pin xmin without appearing as a backend at all. Check pg_prepared_xacts.
<|call:run_sql_query|> {"sql": "SELECT gid, prepared, age(transactionid) FROM pg_prepared_xacts ORDER BY prepared", "db": "analytics"}
System Response: gid='dbt_2pc_4471' prepared 9 days ago, age(transactionid)=410M — orphaned 2PC from a crashed coordinator, pinning the global xmin.
Recovery reasoning: A 9-day-old orphaned prepared transaction holds the xmin horizon and blocks all vacuum reclamation. It is safe to ROLLBACK PREPARED — the coordinator is long dead and no commit will ever arrive.
<|call:run_sql_query|> {"sql": "ROLLBACK PREPARED 'dbt_2pc_4471'", "db": "analytics"}
System Response: ROLLBACK PREPARED ok; xmin horizon advanced 410M; next vacuum reclaimed ~0.9TB of dead tuples; disk 88% -> 41%.
Resolution: Root cause was an orphaned prepared (2PC) transaction from a crashed coordinator pinning the global xmin; rolling it back unblocked vacuum reclamation cluster-wide.

Reasoning: WAL not recycling plus a replica that dropped out points at a replication slot still reserving WAL. First list the replication slots and their retained WAL.
<|call:check_replication_slots|> {"db": "orders"}
System Response: [OK] slot orders_replica_1 active=true, restart_lsn recent, retained_wal=2GB — slot looks healthy and active.
Recovery reasoning: The trap: 2GB retained cannot explain 380GB of WAL, and one healthy slot may be masking another. Compute per-slot WAL reservation against the current LSN across ALL slots, including logical ones.
<|call:run_sql_query|> {"sql": "SELECT slot_name, slot_type, active, pg_size_pretty(pg_wal_lsn_diff(pg_current_wal_lsn(), restart_lsn)) AS retained FROM pg_replication_slots ORDER BY 4 DESC", "db": "orders"}
System Response: slot debezium_cdc (logical) active=false retained=372GB inactive_since=14h; orders_replica_1 retained=2GB — the dead CDC slot holds 372GB.
Recovery reasoning: An orphaned logical CDC slot (Debezium connector died 14h ago) pins 372GB of WAL. Dropping it frees WAL at the next checkpoint; the connector re-snapshots on reconnect, so no data loss — confirm it is the inactive one before dropping.
<|call:drop_replication_slot|> {"db": "orders", "slot": "debezium_cdc", "confirm_inactive": true}
System Response: slot dropped; WAL recycled at next checkpoint; pg_wal 380GB -> 18GB; disk 92% -> 44%.
Resolution: Root cause was an orphaned inactive logical (Debezium) replication slot pinning 372GB of WAL after the connector died; dropping it let WAL recycle while the healthy physical slot was untouched.

Reasoning: Same index and same plan but 50x buffer reads means the index itself bloated with dead/empty pages. First confirm the plan really uses the index and capture buffer counts.
<|call:explain_query|> {"db": "search", "sql": "SELECT id FROM users WHERE email=$1", "analyze": true, "buffers": true}
System Response: [OK] Index Scan using users_email_idx; Buffers: shared hit=4 read=1190; rows=1 — plan is correct and uses the index.
Recovery reasoning: The trap: 'plan is correct' looks fine, but reading ~1190 pages to return one row is the signature of a bloated btree, not a plan problem. Quantify the index bloat before rebuilding.
<|call:check_index_bloat|> {"db": "search", "index": "users_email_idx"}
System Response: index size 21GB, est. live 3.4GB, bloat 84%, leaf density 16% — heavy bloat from a year of non-HOT UPDATE churn.
Recovery reasoning: 84% bloat explains the buffer blowup. Rebuild without blocking writes — REINDEX CONCURRENTLY keeps the table writable throughout, preserving availability.
<|call:reindex_concurrently|> {"db": "search", "index": "users_email_idx"}
System Response: REINDEX CONCURRENTLY done in 11m; index 21GB -> 3.4GB; buffers read 1190 -> 6; latency 140ms -> 2ms.
Resolution: Root cause was 84% btree bloat in users_email_idx from non-HOT UPDATE churn; REINDEX CONCURRENTLY rebuilt it online with no write downtime, restoring 2ms lookups.

Reasoning: The ALTER dies exactly at the statement_timeout boundary while reads stall behind it — that smells like a lock-queue problem, not a slow ALTER. First look at the live blocking tree around users during an attempt.
<|call:check_blocking_tree|> {"db": "users", "around_relation": "users"}
System Response: [OK] no DML writer is blocking anything; only the migrator (pid 7780, ALTER TABLE users) is waiting, with no upstream blocker listed.
Recovery reasoning: The trap: 'no blocker' makes the DB look idle and the ALTER merely slow, tempting the wrong fix of raising statement_timeout. But a pending AccessExclusiveLock request queues every reader behind it. Check the migrator's timeout settings.
<|call:check_pg_settings|> {"db": "users", "session_role": "migrator", "keys": ["statement_timeout", "lock_timeout"]}
System Response: statement_timeout=30s, lock_timeout=0 (disabled) — the migrator never yields its place in the lock queue.
Recovery reasoning: With lock_timeout=0 the ALTER sits at the head of the lock queue for the full 30s, blocking every reader behind it. Set a short lock_timeout so it grabs the lock only if instantly available; the column add has no default, so it is metadata-only and instant once it holds the lock.
<|call:set_config_param|> {"db": "users", "session_role": "migrator", "params": {"lock_timeout": "200ms", "statement_timeout": "0"}}
System Response: applied; retried ALTER acquired the lock in <50ms, added the column (no default) instantly; reads never queued; migration committed.
Resolution: Root cause was lock_timeout=0 letting the ALTER's AccessExclusiveLock request block all readers for the full statement_timeout window; a 200ms lock_timeout made the DDL yield instead of queue, applying the metadata-only column with zero read downtime.

Reasoning: A sudden total stall right after a DDL is a lock-queue pileup: the ALTER wants AccessExclusiveLock and everything queues behind it. First get the blocking tree to find the head of the chain.
<|call:check_blocking_tree|> {"db": "catalog", "around_relation": "products"}
System Response: [ERROR] statement timeout: query over pg_locks/pg_stat_activity exceeded 60s — the monitoring query itself is queued behind the pileup.
Recovery reasoning: The trap: even the diagnostic is stuck. Bypass it with a superuser emergency connection and a tiny statement_timeout so the probe cannot get caught in the queue.
<|call:run_sql_query|> {"db": "catalog", "sql": "SET LOCAL statement_timeout='2s'; SELECT pid, wait_event, age(now(),xact_start) FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE state<>'idle' ORDER BY xact_start LIMIT 3", "connection": "superuser_emergency"}
System Response: pid 3301 active 47m query=SELECT ... FROM products p JOIN ... (analytics export); pid 7780 ALTER products waiting on 3301; 398 sessions waiting on 7780.
Recovery reasoning: A 47-minute analytics export holds AccessShareLock on products; the ALTER is stuck behind it and the whole app is stuck behind the ALTER. Cancel the root holder — cancel, not terminate, lets it unwind cleanly and the ALTER then proceeds.
<|call:cancel_backend|> {"db": "catalog", "pid": 3301, "reason": "47m analytics export heading a DDL lock pileup"}
System Response: pg_cancel_backend(3301) ok; ALTER acquired the lock and committed in 300ms; 398 queued sessions drained; throughput restored in 15s.
Resolution: Root cause was a 47-minute analytics SELECT holding AccessShareLock at the head of a queue behind the queued ALTER's AccessExclusiveLock; canceling the export let the DDL and all blocked sessions proceed with no terminated app connections.

Reasoning: All access to one table hanging right after a reclaim job — first check whether that job is simply making progress and needs to finish, by looking at the disk trend.
<|call:check_disk_usage|> {"host": "db-reporting-prod", "path": "/pgdata"}
System Response: [OK] /pgdata 71% and falling (-3%/min) — the reclaim job appears to be working and freeing space.
Recovery reasoning: The trap: falling disk is misleading — that is VACUUM FULL writing a compacted copy while holding AccessExclusiveLock on the table, which is exactly why every read hangs. Confirm the lock holder.
<|call:check_pg_locks|> {"db": "reporting", "relation": "daily_rollup", "mode": "AccessExclusiveLock"}
System Response: pid 5120 holds AccessExclusiveLock, query=VACUUM (FULL) daily_rollup, held 8m, 60 waiters queued.
Recovery reasoning: VACUUM FULL exclusive-locks for the whole rewrite. Canceling is corruption-safe — it builds a new relfile and only swaps at commit, so a cancel rolls back to the intact original. Cancel it, then reclaim online instead.
<|call:cancel_backend|> {"db": "reporting", "pid": 5120, "reason": "VACUUM FULL exclusive-locking daily_rollup, blocking all reads"}
System Response: pg_cancel_backend ok; VACUUM FULL rolled back, original heap intact; reads on daily_rollup resumed immediately.
Recovery reasoning: Reads are back but the bloat that triggered the cron remains. Replace the blocking VACUUM FULL with an online compaction that holds only a brief lock at the swap.
<|call:pg_repack_table|> {"db": "reporting", "table": "daily_rollup", "schedule": "next_low_traffic_window"}
System Response: pg_repack queued for 02:30; est. reclaim 60GB with only a momentary AccessExclusiveLock at swap.
Resolution: Root cause was an overnight VACUUM FULL holding AccessExclusiveLock for a full-table rewrite (the falling disk was the misleading trap); canceling it left the original heap intact and restored reads, with pg_repack scheduled for safe online reclaim.

Reasoning: An advisory lock that nothing visibly holds, behind PgBouncer transaction mode, smells like a leaked session-level advisory lock stranded on a pooled backend. First check who holds advisory lock 42.
<|call:check_advisory_locks|> {"db": "billing", "key": 42}
System Response: [ERROR] query routed via pgbouncer (txn mode) landed on a different backend than the holder; returned 0 advisory locks for key 42.
Recovery reasoning: The trap: the pooler sent my probe to a clean backend, so it falsely reports no lock. Query pg_locks directly on the database, bypassing PgBouncer, across all backends for objid=42.
<|call:run_sql_query|> {"db": "billing", "sql": "SELECT pid, mode, granted FROM pg_locks WHERE locktype='advisory' AND objid=42", "connection": "direct_no_pooler"}
System Response: pid 8123 holds advisory ExclusiveLock objid=42 granted=true; backend state=idle, client gone 3 days — a session-level lock never released.
Recovery reasoning: A session-level pg_advisory_lock(42) leaked onto a pooled backend whose client disconnected 3 days ago; in transaction pooling the session lock outlives the client. Release it, and switch the job to an xact-scoped advisory lock that auto-releases at commit under PgBouncer.
<|call:run_sql_query|> {"db": "billing", "sql": "SELECT pg_terminate_backend(8123)", "connection": "direct_no_pooler"}
System Response: backend 8123 terminated; advisory lock 42 released; reconciler acquired pg_advisory_xact_lock(42) and ran; future runs auto-release at txn end.
Resolution: Root cause was a session-level advisory lock leaked onto a PgBouncer-pooled backend (incompatible with transaction pooling); terminating the stranded backend freed it, and moving to pg_advisory_xact_lock guarantees release at transaction end, preserving the single-runner invariant.

Reasoning: A periodic ~3-day read-IO storm with no deploy and no dead-tuple churn on a big append-only table is the signature of an anti-wraparound freeze pass scanning the whole heap. First check the freeze-age trend on metrics_2026.
<|call:check_txid_age|> {"db": "timeseries", "table": "metrics_2026", "fields": ["relfrozenxid_age", "last_autovacuum"]}
System Response: [OK] relfrozenxid_age=11M, last_autovacuum 9 min ago, last run scanned 4 pages — table looks freshly and cheaply vacuumed.
Recovery reasoning: The trap: right now we are between storms, so the live age looks tame and the last vacuum was tiny. The real question is what vacuum does at peak — pull the autovacuum history over the last week.
<|call:check_autovacuum_status|> {"db": "timeseries", "table": "metrics_2026", "history_window": "7d"}
System Response: every ~72h an 'to prevent wraparound' autovacuum runs, scans all 90M pages (the full 700GB heap), 38m duration, heap_blks_read ~ entire table — that is the IO storm.
Recovery reasoning: It is the anti-wraparound freeze re-reading the whole append-only heap every ~3 days because almost no pages get marked all-frozen between passes. Make freezing incremental via insert-triggered autovacuums so the visibility map gets frozen bits as data lands, shrinking each freeze scan — no exclusive lock involved.
<|call:set_config_param|> {"db": "timeseries", "table": "metrics_2026", "params": {"autovacuum_vacuum_insert_scale_factor": 0.0, "autovacuum_vacuum_insert_threshold": 100000, "vacuum_freeze_min_age": 0, "autovacuum_vacuum_cost_delay": 2}}
System Response: applied; subsequent freeze passes scan only recently-inserted pages; next cycle the read-IOPS spike is gone and latency stays flat.
Resolution: Root cause was the anti-wraparound freeze re-scanning the entire 700GB append-only heap every ~3 days because pages were not being frozen incrementally; insert-triggered autovacuums now set frozen bits as data arrives, eliminating the periodic IO storm with no locking impact.

Reasoning: Slowness plus deadlocks that began with a parent-updating trigger point at FK row-lock contention on the parent orders row. First capture the exact conflict from a recent deadlock.
<|call:run_sql_query|> {"db": "orders", "sql": "SELECT detail FROM pg_log WHERE message LIKE 'deadlock detected%' AND ts>now()-interval '10 min' ORDER BY ts DESC LIMIT 1"}
System Response: [OK] 0 rows in the last 10 minutes — no recent deadlock captured.
Recovery reasoning: The trap: an empty 10-minute window does not exonerate the trigger — these deadlocks are bursty. Inspect the live lock modes held on the parent table directly to see the conflict.
<|call:check_pg_locks|> {"db": "orders", "relation": "orders", "group_by": "mode"}
System Response: many backends hold ForKeyShareLock (child inserts) AND ForNoKeyUpdateLock (the total_cents trigger) on the SAME parent rows, while UPDATE orders SET status also wants ForNoKeyUpdate — mutual waits on hot parent rows.
Recovery reasoning: Each child insert FK-locks the parent FOR KEY SHARE, then the new trigger upgrades that same row to FOR NO KEY UPDATE, colliding with status updates in the opposite order — that is the deadlock and the serialization. Take the synchronous parent UPDATE out of the insert path and maintain the total via a batched aggregate, so inserts need only the cheap FK lock.
<|call:set_config_param|> {"scope": "orders-api", "key": "order_total_strategy", "value": "async_batched_aggregate", "drop_trigger": "trg_order_items_total_cents"}
System Response: trigger dropped; totals now recomputed by a 5s batched job; child inserts take only ForKeyShare; deadlocks 0; insert p99 back to baseline.
Resolution: Root cause was a parent-updating trigger that escalated every FK child insert's KEY SHARE lock into a NO KEY UPDATE on the hot parent row, deadlocking against status updates; moving total maintenance to a batched async aggregate left only the cheap FK lock while keeping totals eventually consistent.

Reasoning: Main table stable but disk ballooning with slow jsonb updates means bloat hiding in the out-of-line TOAST table, not the heap. First confirm the main table's vacuum health to rule it out.
<|call:check_table_stats|> {"db": "docs", "table": "documents", "fields": ["n_dead_tup", "last_autovacuum", "heap_size"]}
System Response: [OK] documents: n_dead_tup=4k, last_autovacuum 6 min ago, heap_size 12GB stable — main table is perfectly healthy.
Recovery reasoning: The trap: a healthy heap is exactly why this is hidden — large jsonb bodies live in the associated TOAST relation, which vacuums on its own thresholds. Inspect the TOAST table's size and dead tuples.
<|call:check_toast_bloat|> {"db": "docs", "table": "documents"}
System Response: pg_toast_16489: size 188GB, n_dead_tup 240M, last_autovacuum 14 days ago — every body UPDATE rewrites TOAST chunks and they are not being reclaimed.
Recovery reasoning: TOAST autovacuum has not run in 14 days because it inherits the coarse default scale_factor against a huge relation, so dead chunks pile up. Vacuum the TOAST table now and tighten its per-table thresholds — online, non-exclusive.
<|call:run_vacuum|> {"db": "docs", "table": "documents", "options": {"toast": true, "analyze": true, "toast_scale_factor": 0.02}}
System Response: VACUUM reclaimed 170GB from pg_toast_16489; toast.autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor set to 0.02; /pgdata recovered 180GB; body UPDATE latency back to baseline.
Resolution: Root cause was bloat in the out-of-line TOAST table for documents.body (dead jsonb chunks unvacuumed for 14 days) while the main heap looked healthy; vacuuming the TOAST relation and tightening its thresholds reclaimed 170GB online with no exclusive lock.
